-- The thread kind `review` becomes `remember`, in the constraint and in the rows.
-- docs/plans/260901d-rename-review-mode-to-remember-mode-everywhere.md
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit (the DROP and the ADD), then the data movement was
-- inserted BETWEEN them. That order is the whole point, and it is the same trap
-- 0041 wrote down: Postgres validates a re-added CHECK against the rows already
-- in the table, so narrowing before the data moves fails with a 23514 — and it
-- fails only where there is history, passing cleanly on an empty container.
-- `drizzle-kit generate` diffs src/db/schema.ts and cannot see a data movement
-- at all, so it will never emit the UPDATE; a reader regenerating this file will
-- get the two statements without it, and that file would be wrong.
--
-- **`review` is the reader-facing mode renamed to Remember**, not code review
-- and not Referee mode. `chat_threads.kind` is the only relational home of the
-- string: `feedback.diagnostics` also stores a mode name, and those rows are
-- deliberately left alone because they are telemetry describing what a reader
-- actually saw. The plan's § *Where the string is persisted* has the survey.
--
-- No compatibility window and no alias, on Greg's licence ("we have no real
-- users yet"). src/routes.ts refuses the old wire value with a 400 rather than
-- accepting both, so this migration and the deploy that follows it are a brief
-- maintenance window rather than an expand/migrate/contract.
--
-- **No postcondition check, unlike 0041's**, and the difference is worth
-- naming: there, two of the four rewrites had no constraint behind them, so
-- "matched nothing" and "worked" printed the same tick. Here the re-added CHECK
-- below IS the postcondition — a single surviving 'review' row makes it fail
-- with 23514, and the whole migration is one transaction, so a refusal leaves
-- the database exactly as it was.
--
-- **`chat_messages.stance` needs no update.** Its values (balanced/respond/
-- socratic/signposts) do not encode the thread kind, and the rows stay attached
-- to the migrated thread by identity.
ALTER TABLE "spideryarn"."chat_threads" DROP CONSTRAINT "chat_threads_kind";--> statement-breakpoint
UPDATE "spideryarn"."chat_threads" SET "kind" = 'remember' WHERE "kind" = 'review';--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "spideryarn"."chat_threads" ADD CONSTRAINT "chat_threads_kind" CHECK ("spideryarn"."chat_threads"."kind" in ('chat','remember'));
